  "body": "I was seeing some weird drift in the new MoveIt Servo, and finally found the source of the issue.\r\n\r\nIn new servo we're doing conversions to the planning frame using a TF buffer:\r\n\r\n```cpp\r\nconst auto planning_to_command_tf =\r\n    transform_buffer_.lookupTransform(command.frame_id, servo_params_.planning_frame, rclcpp::Time(0));\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn the old servo, we're doing conversions to the planning frame using the robot state:\r\n\r\n```cpp\r\nmoveit::core::RobotStatePtr robot_state = planning_scene_monitor_->getStateMonitor()->getCurrentState();\r\nconst Eigen::Isometry3d planning_to_command_tf_eigen =\r\n   robot_state->getGlobalLinkTransform(servo_params_.planning_frame).inverse() *\r\n   robot_state->getGlobalLinkTransform(command.frame_id);\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhile functionally this is the same, it's causing significant drift in these systems.\r\n\r\nNew servo (even after the correction in https://github.com/ros-planning/moveit2/pull/2350):\r\nhttps://github.com/ros-planning/moveit2/assets/4603398/43caf2e6-bb25-42ab-a115-884b57a9c1f6\r\n\r\nOld servo (actually, this is new servo but with the transform approach reverted as above):\r\nhttps://github.com/ros-planning/moveit2/assets/4603398/c4b778fe-59f3-4f12-a94a-9763fed33d49\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\nSo, I think we should have some kind of check on whether the frame name can be retrieved using the robot model or not, and that should decide whether to use TF.\r\n\r\n@AndyZe @ibrahiminfinite ",
